course: dictionary
lesson: 11029
title: Wayfarer: a traveller on foot
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wayfarer is a traveller who goes on foot, walking from place to place. It is an old, literary word, so you will mostly find it in older storybooks, poems and hymns rather than in everyday talk. It is a noun: one wayfarer, many wayfarers.
explain: Use the context to work it out. «He lies in the hedge by the dusty lane, thinking of the road and all the wondrous world that it led to, and of all the wayfarers on it.» The road leads somewhere, the lane is dusty, and people are travelling along it. Try your meaning in the sentence: "all the walkers on it" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: The chapter title helps too. «Wayfarers All» is the name of a chapter in The Wind in the Willows, and the example sentence explains it: wayfarers are travellers on foot, people on their way somewhere. Notice that the word is about a journey, not about where a person lives.
fact: A wayfarer is a traveller who goes on foot, walking from place to place.
fact: «Wayfarer» is an old, literary word, so you will mostly meet it in older books, poems and hymns.
fact: «Wayfarers All» is the name of a chapter in The Wind in the Willows.
fact: In church hymns, life is sometimes called a journey, and the faithful are wayfarers.
fact: The phrase «weary wayfarer» means a tired traveller who has been walking for a long time.
exercise: In the sentence «and of all the wayfarers on it», the road is the place they travel along. Who are the wayfarers? | Clouds moving slowly over the hills | *Travellers walking along the road | Farmers selling apples at the gate | Trees growing beside the lane
exercise: A storybook says, «a weary wayfarer reached the village at dusk.» What is a weary wayfarer? | A wide ship with white sails | A sleepy cat by the fire | *A tired traveller who has walked a long way
exercise: Nadia says wayfarer sounds like a bird that flies. What does wayfarer really mean? | A shop that sells maps | *A traveller who goes on foot, walking from place to place | A bird that flies south for the winter | A boat that sails across the sea
test: Find the word in this sentence that means travellers on foot: «The wayfarers walked on, and the lane wound over the hill.» | lane | wound | *wayfarers | hill
test: Which word means a traveller who walks from place to place? | lantern | *wayfarer | hedge | chapter
exam: Which sentence uses wayfarer correctly? | The wayfarer ate a bowl of soup in the kitchen | *The wayfarer walked from village to village, carrying only a small bag | The wayfarer was a shiny red car | The wayfarer rode his bicycle to the shops
